#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# finite_field_element_trace

#% text
# `finite_field_element_trace(a)` gives the trace of the element `a` of `F_(p^n)` to the prime field: the sum of its conjugates `a + a^p + ... + a^(p^(n-1))`, as an integer from 0 to `p - 1`.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# The trace is linear over the prime field; it is the negated coefficient of `x^(n-1)` in the characteristic polynomial of `a`.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
F = finite_field(3, 4)

#% cell
finite_field_element_trace(F([1, 1, 2]))
#% output
#= 1

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# An element of the prime field has the trace `n a`, here `4 * 2` modulo 3:

#% cell
finite_field_element_trace(finite_field(3, 4)(2))
#% output
#= 2

#% text
# The values over the first nine elements, as the trace is a linear map onto the prime field:

#% cell
table(finite_field_element_trace(finite_field(3, 4)(k)), k, range(0, 8))
#% output
#= [0, 1, 2, 1, 2, 0, 2, 0, 1]

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# The trace is additive:

#% cell
a = F(20)

#% cell
b = F(55)

#% cell
finite_field_element_trace(a + b) == mod(finite_field_element_trace(a) + finite_field_element_trace(b), 3)
#% output
#= true

#% text
# Exactly a third of the elements have trace 0:

#% cell
count(table(finite_field_element_trace(F(k)), k, range(0, 80)), 0)
#% output
#= 27

#% text
# The trace is the sum of the conjugates `a`, `a^3`, `a^9` and `a^27`, taken in the field:

#% cell
finite_field(3, 4)([1, 1, 2]) + finite_field(3, 4)([1, 1, 2])^3 + finite_field(3, 4)([1, 1, 2])^9 + finite_field(3, 4)([1, 1, 2])^27 == finite_field(3, 4)(finite_field_element_trace(finite_field(3, 4)([1, 1, 2])))
#% output
#= true

#% subsection
# Possible issues

#% text
# The argument must be a finite field element; an integer stays unevaluated:

#% cell
finite_field_element_trace(3)
#% output
#= finite_field_element_trace(3)

#% section
# See also

#% text
